\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory I Study Guide
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This study guide provides an overview of all the topics covered during the
 Fall 2012 semester of Macroeconomic Theory I, Econ 636.
 It outlines the lecture notes, theorems, proofs, and other material covered
 during the course.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Macroeconomics studies aggregate variables, such as aggregate consumtion,
 aggregate investment, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Questions are typically dynamic, and hence, dynamic programming is useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Today, many macro models have micro foundations, i.e., models where behavior
 is derived from basic assumptions about consumers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There are two types of macro models: one generation where agents are infinitely
 lived, and multiple generations where agents are finitely lived.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There are two types of timing models: continuous time and discrete time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this class, we analyze discrete time models where agents are infinitely
 lived.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are three steps used in studying Macroeconomics:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Set up of macro models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use mathematical or numerical techniques, or a combination of the two, to
 solve the models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Extract real life data and make comparisons with the predictions obtained
 from the model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A Finite Horizon Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Social Planner's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we present a discrete time, finite horizon example of the
 neoclassic growth model, also known as the Solow growth model.
 It is important to note that this finite horizon model is only being shown
 for illustration since finite horizon models are not typically used for
 macroeconomic analysis.
 In this model, there is no government and there are no exports.
 Here, we present a version of the social planner's problem in which there
 is no market where individuals trade.
 The constrained optimization problem is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\left\{ c_{t},k_{t}\right\} _{t=0}^{T}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}\leq F(k_{t},L_{t})+(1-\delta)k_{t};\ \forall\ t=0,1,\ldots,T\\
 & c_{t}\geq0;\ \forall\ t=0,1,\ldots,T\\
 & k_{t+1}\geq0;\ \forall\ t=0,1,\ldots,T\\
 & 0\leq L_{t}\leq1;\ \forall\ t=0,1,\ldots,T\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is the called the discount factor, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 is the depreciation rate, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 indicates the time period.
 In each period, there is only one good, either for consumption or for investmen
t.
 We provide a brief explanation of the variables and functions that characterize
 the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}:$
\end_inset

 The amount of goods consumed in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Note that each 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is the same good, but since we are maximizing utility over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 periods, each 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 is distinct from the others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}:$
\end_inset

 The amount of capital available next period, 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the amount of goods invested in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}$
\end_inset

, will always be turned into future capital.
 So, the total amount of capital available in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 equals the amount of goods invested in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 plus the usable capital from the last period.
 This situation is expressed by the equation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i_{t}=k_{t+1}-(1-\delta)k_{t}\iff k_{t+1}=i_{t}+(1-\delta)k_{t}
\]

\end_inset

In this setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 can also be thought of as the amount of capital saved for next period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t},L_{t}:$
\end_inset

 the amount of capital and labor in available in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $F(k_{t},L_{t}):$
\end_inset

 This is the production function which uses 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 as inputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\delta)k_{t}:$
\end_inset

 The undepreciated capital available in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Feasibility constraint: Consumption and investment in a given period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 cannot exceed the amount of goods produced in that period, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t}+i_{t} & \leq & F(k_{t},L_{t})\\
c_{t}+(k_{t+1}-(1-\delta)k_{t}) & \leq & F(k_{t},L_{t})\\
c_{t}+k_{t+1} & \leq & F(k_{t},L_{t})+(1-\delta)k_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

It does not matter whether the constraint has an equality or a strict inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the amount of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

leisure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 does not affect utility, then the amount of labor used in production does
 not matter, so we will set 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 to increase output.
 So, define 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k_{t})\equiv F(k_{t},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing, differentiable, and concave.
 
\begin_inset Formula $F(k,1)$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing, differentiable, and concave.
 
\begin_inset Formula $F(0,L)=F(k,0)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, since 
\begin_inset Formula $F(k,1)$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing, differentiable, and concave, then so is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k)$
\end_inset

.
 Second, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}+k_{t+1}<f(k_{t})$
\end_inset

 cannot be optimal.
 The weak inequality just enlarges the feasibility set, but at the optimal,
 the social planner will make the equation an equality since he does not
 want to waste resources.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=f(k_{t})-k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have what is known as the 
\emph on
Ramsey problem
\emph default
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\left\{ k_{t+1}\right\} _{t=0}^{T}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & 0\leq k_{t+1}\leq f(k_{t});\ \forall\ t=0,\ldots,T\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is one specific setup of the neoclassic growth model.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 is essentially given (it signifies the capital saved from previous periods),
 then to obtain the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

, we must choose the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 The constraint qualification condition (the gradients of binding constraints
 are linearly independent) is not easily verifiable as we do not know which
 ones bind in general.
 To overcome this problem, economists use a simple trick.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption

\series bold
\shape up
(Inada condition on utility).

\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{c\rightarrow0}}\ u^{\prime}(c)=\infty$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Inada condition says that zero consumption is undesirable since marginal
 utility is very high when 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is increased by a small amount.
 Hence, consumption in all periods must be positive.
 For example, CRRA utility functions meet this condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
Under the Inada condition on utility, the optimal solution is interior,
 that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{T+1}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $0<k_{t+1}<f(k_{t})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First, we show that capital must be positive.
 Suppose for contradiction that this is not true.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 be the period when capital starts to be zero, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Fix other capitals and only vary 
\begin_inset Formula $x=k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, then the objective function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ldots+\beta^{t}u(f(k_{t})-x)+\beta^{t+1}u(f(x))+\ldots
\]

\end_inset

The derivative of the above function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(f(k_{t})-x)+\beta^{t+1}u^{\prime}(f(x))f^{\prime}(x)
\]

\end_inset

which is infinity at 
\begin_inset Formula $x=0$
\end_inset

 by the Inada condition.
 This is a contradiction because the Kuhn-Tucker conditions are never satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we show that consumption must be positive.
 Suppose for contradiciton that this is not true.
 Then, pick the last period when consumption is zero, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+1}>0$
\end_inset

.
 The derivative is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(f(k_{t})-x)+\beta^{t+1}u^{\prime}(f(x)-k_{t+2})f^{\prime}(x)
\]

\end_inset

which is negative infinity when 
\begin_inset Formula $x=f(k_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction because the Kuhn-Tucker conditions are never satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the Lagrangian of the optimization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})
\]

\end_inset

and the first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial k_{t+1}}=-\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})+\beta^{t+1}u^{\prime}(f(k_{t+1})-k_{t+1})f^{\prime}(k_{t+1})=0;\ \forall\ t=0,\ldots,T-1
\]

\end_inset

The summary statement of the first-order conditions is then the Euler equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u^{\prime}(c_{t})=\beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})f^{\prime}(k_{t+1});\ \forall\ t=0,\ldots,T-1\\
k_{T+1}=0,\ k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The economics intuition of the Euler equation is as follows: the utility
 lose if you invest in one more unit, i.e., the marginal coast of saving (LHS)
 equals the discounted utility increase next period per unit of increase
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 times the return on the invested unit, i.e., the marginal benefit of saving
 (RHS).
 Also, there are a few things that we should note about the Euler equation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The number of equations and unknowns are 
\begin_inset Formula $T+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
It is a second order difference equation because there are two lags of capital.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The first-order conditions are sufficient if the objective function is concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see the concavity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=0}^{T}\beta^{t}u(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})$
\end_inset

, it is sufficient to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is concave for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, as the sume of concave functions is still concave.
 We have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial u}{\partial k_{t}} & = & u^{\prime}(c_{t})f^{\prime}(k_{t})\\
\frac{\partial u}{\partial k_{t+1}} & = & -u^{\prime}(c_{t})\\
\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t}^{2}} & = & u^{\prime\prime}(c_{t})(f^{\prime}(k_{t}))^{2}+u^{\prime}(c_{t})f^{\prime\prime}(k_{t})\leq0\\
\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t+1}^{2}} & = & u^{\prime\prime}(c_{t})\leq0\\
\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t}\partial k_{t+1}} & = & -u^{\prime\prime}(c_{t})f^{\prime}(k_{t})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Hence, the Hessian matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t}^{2}}\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t+1}^{2}}-\left(\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial k_{t}\partial k_{t+1}}\right)^{2}=u^{\prime}(c_{t})f^{\prime\prime}(k_{t})u^{\prime\prime}(c_{t})\geq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example

\emph on
(Numerical exploration) 
\emph default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)=\log c$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k)=k^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $T=3$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{0}=2$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=0.1$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{k_{0}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{0}-k_{1}} & = & \frac{\beta\left(\alpha k_{1}^{\alpha-1}+1-\delta\right)}{k_{1}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{1}-k_{2}}\\
\frac{1}{k_{1}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{1}-k_{2}} & = & \frac{\beta\left(\alpha k_{2}^{\alpha-1}+1-\delta\right)}{k_{2}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{2}-k_{3}}\\
\frac{1}{k_{2}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{2}-k_{3}} & = & \frac{\beta\left(\alpha k_{3}^{\alpha-1}+1-\delta\right)}{k_{3}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)k_{3}-k_{4}}\\
k_{4} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If you know 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset

, you can solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset

, and so on.
 At then end, you must have 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{4}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the example above, you would use numerical methods to solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\{k_{t}\}_{t=0}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example

\emph on
(Analytical exploration)
\emph default
 Let us keep the same setup from the previous example, except now 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k)=Ak+(1-\delta)k=(A+(1-\delta))k=Rk$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Solve the linear difference equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{Rk_{t}-k_{t+1}} & = & \frac{\beta R}{Rk_{t+1}-k_{t+2}};\ t=0,\ldots,T-1\\
k_{t+2}+Rk_{t+1}-\beta R(k_{t+1}-Rk_{t}) & = & 0;\ t=0,\ldots,T-1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The characteristic equation is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda^{2}-R\lambda-\beta R(\lambda-R) & = & 0\\
(\lambda-\beta R)(\lambda-R) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

so its two roots are 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}=R$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}=\beta R$
\end_inset

.
 So the solution takes the form of
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t}=AR^{t}+B(\beta R)^{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are undetermined parameters.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{T+1}=0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
AR^{T+1}+B(\beta R)^{T+1} & = & 0\\
A+B\beta^{T+1} & = & 0\\
A & = & -B\beta^{T+1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A+B=k_{0}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

, then we obtain the following equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
A & = & \frac{-\beta^{T+1}}{1-\beta^{T+1}}k_{0}\\
B & = & \frac{k_{0}}{1-\beta^{T+1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Finally, plugging these expressions into the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t} & = & Rk_{t}-k_{t+1}\\
 & = & R(AR^{t}+B(\beta R)^{t})-AR^{t+1}-B(\beta R)^{t+1}\\
 & = & (B\beta^{t}-B\beta^{t+1})R^{t+1}\\
 & = & \frac{k_{0}(1-\beta)\beta^{t}R^{t+1}}{1-\beta^{T+1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Consolidate the 
\begin_inset Formula $T+1$
\end_inset

 feasibility constraints and then solve for the multiplier conditions.
 First, multiply the equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[c_{t}+k_{t+1}=Rk_{t}\right]$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{-t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{0}+k_{1} & = & Rk_{0}\\
c_{1}R^{-1}+k_{2}R^{-1} & = & k_{1}\\
c_{2}R^{-2}+k_{3}R^{-2} & = & k_{2}R^{-1}\\
 & \vdots\\
c_{T}R^{-T}+K_{T+1}R^{-T} & = & Rk_{T}R^{-T}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Substracting common terms from the equations and taking summation we obtain
 the single feasibility constraint
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}R^{-t}c_{t}=Rk_{0}
\]

\end_inset

where the LHS is the present-value of consumption stream, and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is the rate of return form saving capital.
 Note, if we consolidated the constraints starting at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=s$
\end_inset

, then we would have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=s}^{T}}R^{-(t-s)}c_{t}=Rk_{s}
\]

\end_inset

 Thus, starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

, the Lagrangian of the constrained optimization problem using this new
 feasibility constraint is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}\ln c_{t}+\lambda\left(Rk_{0}-{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}R^{-t}c_{t}-K_{T+1}R^{-T}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial c_{t}} & = & \frac{\beta}{c_{t}}-\lambda R^{-t}=0\\
c_{t} & = & \frac{(\beta R)^{t}}{\lambda}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we can obtain an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\frac{\beta^{t}}{\lambda} & = & Rk_{0}\\
\lambda & = & \frac{\sum_{t=0}^{T}\beta^{t}}{Rk_{0}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t} & = & \frac{Rk_{0}(\beta R)^{t}}{\sum_{t=0}^{T}\beta^{t}}\\
 & = & \frac{Rk_{0}}{\frac{1-\beta^{T+1}}{1-\beta}}\beta^{t}R^{t}\\
 & = & \frac{Rk_{0}(1-\beta)\beta^{t}R^{t}}{1-\beta^{T+1}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=0}^{T}\beta^{t}=\frac{1-\beta^{T+1}}{1-\beta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Equilibrium With Sequential Trade
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this subsection, we turn our attention to the decentralized market economy.
 We will show that the allocation achieved in decentralized trade will be
 the same as the allocation in the social planner's problem.
 In the social planner's economy, all variables were aggregate, while the
 decentralized market economy has individual and aggregate variables.
 For the decentralized market economy, we assume that there is a continuum
 of identical consumers, and each decision maker is a price-taker that has
 little influence on aggregate variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Markets and Their Participants
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Static Markets
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Goods Market: the price of the consumption good is normalized to 1, so it
 known as the numeraire.
 In this market, firms are sellers and households are buyers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Labor Market: the wage rate is 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is the price of labor at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 relative to (in terms of) consumption goods at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 In this market, households are sellers and firms are buyers.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Dynamic Market
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Financial Market: This market links periods together.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the real rate of return from saving 
\begin_inset Formula $(t-1\ \mbox{to}\ t)$
\end_inset

.
 In this market, households are sellers and firms are buyers.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Consumer's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As was stated previously, the economy has a continuum of individuals who
 are identical.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{t}^{s}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}L_{t}^{s}(i)di
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t}^{s}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}k_{t}^{s}(i)di
\]

\end_inset

Hence, this is a representative-agent economy, so we can describe all the
 consumers in the economy using a representative agent.
 Also, this is a perfect foresight model, which means that individuals know
 what the future prices will be when making their decisions in period zero.
 Thus, the representative agent solves his maximization problem at period
 zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This consumer wishes to maximize his utility subject to his budget constraint.
 So, his constrained maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle {\displaystyle \max_{\{k_{t+1}^{s},n_{t}^{s},c_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}^{s}\leq w_{t}n_{t}^{s}+R_{t}k_{t}^{s};\ \forall\ t=0,\ldots,T\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{s}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of labor supplied by the consumer in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{s}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of funds supplied to the financial market by the consumer
 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

.
 The right side of the consumer's budget constraint represents his income.
 The Lagrangian is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})+\sum_{t=0}^{T}\lambda_{t}(w_{t}n_{t}^{s}+R_{t}k_{t}^{s}-c_{t}-k_{t+1}^{s})
\]

\end_inset

and the first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial c_{t}}=0:\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})=\lambda_{t}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial k_{t+1}^{s}}=0:\lambda_{t}=\lambda_{t+1}R_{t+1}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, we can obtain the Euler equation for the consumer
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*})=\beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{*})
\]

\end_inset

Since the household can be thought of as the stockholder, or owner, of the
 firm, profit from the firm should also be included in the consumer's income.
 However, in a competitive equilibrium, the firm's profit equals zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Firm's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The firm wishes to maximize its total profit per period.
 So, its maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d}}}\ F(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{d}-w_{t}n_{t}^{d}-R_{t}k_{t}^{d}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of labor employed by the firm in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is the capital borrowed by the firm in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order conditions of the firm are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{t} & = & F_{k}(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})+(1-\delta)\\
w_{t} & = & F_{n}(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In this setup, we focus on the case where the production function satisfies
 constant returns to scale, so it is homogenous of degree one, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda>0:F(\lambda k,\lambda L)=\lambda F(k,L)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a mathematical result says we can write the production function as
 
\begin_inset Formula $F(k,L)=F_{k}\cdot k+F_{L}\cdot L$
\end_inset

.
 Under CRS, the firm's profit equals zero in a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The number of firms is undetermined as long as we have many small firms
 and the price-taking behavior can be justified.
 Because of CRS, having 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 firms is equivalent to having 1 firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Characterizing the Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium is a sequence of prices and allocations such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
All the decision makers maximize their utility and profit, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Supply equals demand in every market.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The allocation is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{*},k_{t}^{s*},k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{s*},n_{t}^{d*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}^{*},w_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 denote the competitive equilibrium allocations and prices, respectively.
 Thus, by the definition above, we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
c_{t}^{*},k_{t+1}^{s*},n_{t}^{s*}\in\arg\max\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ c_{t}+k_{t+1}^{s}=R_{t}^{*}k_{t}+w_{t}^{*}n_{t}\\
k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{d*}\in\arg\max\ F(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{d}-w_{t}^{*}n_{t}^{d}-R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{d}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{s*}=n_{t}^{d*}=n_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{d*}=k_{t}^{s*}=k_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Demand equals supply in the goods market, and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{*}+k_{t+1}^{s*}=F(k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{d*})+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{d*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us analyze the conditions that characterize a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{s*}=n_{t}^{d*}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
First-order condition for a consumer
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*}) & = & \lambda_{t}\\
u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*}) & = & \beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{*})R_{t+1}^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
First-order condition for the firm
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{t}^{*} & = & F_{k}(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)\\
w_{t}^{*} & = & F_{n}(k_{t}^{*},1)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
CRS implies that the firm's profit equals zero.
 To show this, consider the firm's profit function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
F(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{*}-R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{*}-w_{t}^{*}\\
=F(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{*}-\left[F_{k}(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)\right]k_{t}^{*}-F_{n}(k_{t}^{*},1)\\
=\left[F_{k}(k_{t}^{*},1)\cdot k_{t}^{*}+F_{n}(k_{t}^{*},1)\right]+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{*}-\left[F_{k}(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)\right]k_{t}^{*}-F_{n}(k_{t}^{*},1)\\
=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The consumer's budget constraint is satisfied if and only if the feasibility
 constraint in the social planner's problem is satisfied.
 Since profits equal zero, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{*}=R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{*}+w_{t}^{*}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t}^{*}+k_{t+1}^{*} & = & R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{*}+w_{t}^{*}\\
 & = & F(k_{t}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Euler equation in the social planner's problem holds, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*}) & = & \beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{*})R_{t+1}^{*}\\
u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*}) & = & \beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{*})\left[F_{k}(k_{t+1}^{*},1)+(1-\delta)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The allocation for a CE is feasible in the social planner's problem, i.e.,
 it satisfies the resource constraint, and it satsfies the Euler equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $K_{T+1}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(First Welfare Theorem).
 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{k_{t}^{*},L_{t}^{*},c_{t}^{*}\}_{t=0}^{T}$
\end_inset

 be the allocation in an arbitrary competitive equilibrium, then it is Pareto
 efficient, i.e.
 it solves the social planner's problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Because there is a difference between the aggregate variables and individual
 variables, the consumer's budget constraint is not the same as the feasibility
 condition in the social planner's problem.
 For the consumer, the budget constraint is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is an individual variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 are fixed when 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 changes, and the marginal return of 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 On the other hand, the feasibility constraint for the social planner is
 typically non-linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is an aggregate variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 vary when 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}$
\end_inset

 changes, and the marginal return is 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{k}(K_{t},1)+(1-\delta)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)=\log(c)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k)=k^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $T=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{0}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\frac{2}{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The social planner's feasibility set is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (c_{0},c_{1}):c_{1}\leq(k_{0}^{\alpha}-c_{0})^{\alpha}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The solution is 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}=\frac{1}{1+\alpha\beta}k_{0}^{\alpha}=\frac{3}{4}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}=\frac{\alpha\beta}{1+\alpha\beta}k_{0}^{\alpha}=\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}=k_{1}^{\alpha}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}=\alpha k_{1}^{\alpha-1}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(1-\alpha)k_{1}^{\alpha}=\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The consumer's budset set is a triangle 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (c_{0},c_{1}):c_{0}+c_{1}\leq1+w_{1}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(Second Welfare Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
If an allocation is Pareto efficient, then we can find prices such that
 the prices and the allocation constitute a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The equivalence of the FWT and the SWT relies on the fact there are no distortio
ns in the economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Infinite Horizon Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Why should macroeconomists study the case of an infinite time horizon?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Evolution of the capital is distorted at the end of the model in the finite
 version
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Simplicity: Dynamic programming makes the model easier to solve.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is called the discount factor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This infinite sum is not necessarily convergent.
 Under what conditions will the sum converge?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Preference Requirements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One general condition that guarantees the infinite sum will converge is
 to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta<1$
\end_inset

 and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u(c_{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 is a bounded sequence, i.e., there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $B>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $|u(c_{t})|\leq B\ \forall\ t$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left|{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\right| & \leq & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}|u(c_{t})|\\
 & \leq & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}B\\
 & = & \frac{B}{1-\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

For another example of the infinite sum converging, consider a plan specifying
 a constant flow of consumption:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(\bar{c})
\]

\end_inset

which is convergent if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta<1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively, consider a constantly increasing consumption stream:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{t}=c_{0}(1+\gamma)^{t}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In some models, we will look at
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(c_{t})=\begin{cases}
\frac{c_{t}^{1-\sigma}-1}{1-\sigma} & \mbox{if}\ \sigma\neq1\\
\log c & \mbox{if}\ \sigma=0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

which is constant relative risk aversion (CRRA) utility function.
 So, the model has the following setup
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[\frac{\left[c_{0}(1+\gamma)^{t}\right]^{1-\sigma}-1}{1-\sigma}\right]\\
={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\left[\beta(1+\gamma)^{1-\sigma}\right]^{t}\frac{c_{0}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

In the case of CES utility representation, boundedness required 
\begin_inset Formula $0<|\beta(1+\gamma)^{1-\sigma}|<1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Neoclassic Growth Model: The Social Planner's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We keep the same basic setup of the neoclassic, or Solow, growth model presented
 in the previous section.
 So, we have the 
\emph on
Ramsey problem
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}=f(k_{t});\ \forall\ t\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f(k_{t})\equiv F(k_{t},1)+(1-\delta)k_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Remember, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c_{t})>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime\prime}(c_{t})<0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{c_{t}\rightarrow0}u^{\prime}(c_{t})=\infty$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime}(k_{t})>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime\prime}(k_{t})<0$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, if 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c_{t})$
\end_inset

 is a bounded function, then the sum will converge.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c_{t})$
\end_inset

 being bounded is only a sufficient condition for convergence, so the sum
 can still converge even if 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c_{t})$
\end_inset

 is unbounded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are also sufficient conditions to guarantee that an allocation derived
 in the social planner's is optimal.
 Under additional provisions, these conditions are also necessary, but in
 general, we don't know if this is true and it is difficult to show.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
If a feasible allocation in the social planner's problem, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{k_{t+1}^{*}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, satisfies
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
The Euler equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\prime}(f(k_{t}^{*})-k_{t+1}^{*})=\beta u^{\prime}(f(k_{t+1}^{*})-k_{t+2}^{*})f^{\prime}(k_{t+1}^{*});\ \forall\ t
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A transversality condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*})k_{t+1}^{*}=0
\]

\end_inset

(an intuitive explanation of this condition: the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

value of savings
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is converging to zero as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Theorem
then it is optimal, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\{k_{t+1}^{*}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 solves the social planner's problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The proof of this theorem can be found in Stokey, Lucas, and Prescott, denoted
 Theorem 4.15, on page 98.
 Here, we provide a brief outline it:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u(f(k_{t})-k_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $(k_{t},k_{t+1})$
\end_inset

, then after taking the Taylor expansion of the function around 
\begin_inset Formula $(k_{t}^{*},k_{t+1}^{*})$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u(f(k_{0})-k_{1}) & \leq & u(c_{0}^{*})-u^{\prime}(c_{0}^{*})(k_{1}-k_{1}^{*})\\
\beta u(f(k_{1})-k_{2}) & \leq & \beta u(c_{1}^{*})+\beta u^{\prime}(c_{1}^{*})f^{\prime}(k_{1}^{*})(k_{1}-k_{1}^{*})-\beta u^{\prime}(c_{1}^{*})(k_{2}-k_{2}^{*})\\
 & \vdots\\
\beta^{T}u(f(k_{T})-k_{T+1}) & \leq & \beta^{T}u(c_{T}^{*})+\beta^{T}u^{\prime}(c_{T}^{*})f^{\prime}(k_{T})(k_{T}-k_{T}^{*})-\beta^{T}u^{\prime}(c_{T}^{*})(k_{T+1}-k_{T+1}^{*})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
By using the Euler equation and taking summation of the inequalities, we
 obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}) & \leq & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}^{*})-\beta^{T}u^{\prime}(c_{T}^{*})(k_{T+1}-k_{T+1}^{*})\\
 & \leq & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}^{*})+\beta^{T}u^{\prime}(c_{T}^{*})k_{T+1}^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $T\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 yields the desired result since 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\beta^{T}u^{\prime}(c_{T}^{*})k_{T+1}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Example
Let us consider the case of Cobb-Douglas transition and logarithmic preferences.
 This example is used widely for applied purposes because it has an analytic
 (closed form) solution.
 We wish to find the optimal allocation that solves the following social
 planner's problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{\{k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}} & {\displaystyle {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}}\beta^{t}\log(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}=Ak_{t}^{\alpha}\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that we can substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=Ak_{t}^{\alpha}-k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 into the utility function.
 We will look at the policy 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}=\alpha\beta Ak_{t}^{\alpha}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
The Euler equation for this problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{(1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t}^{\alpha}} & = & \frac{\beta\alpha Ak_{t+1}^{\alpha-1}}{(1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t+1}^{\alpha}}\\
(1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t+1}^{\alpha} & = & \beta\alpha Ak_{t+1}^{\alpha-1}(1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t}^{\alpha}\\
k_{t+1} & = & \alpha\beta Ak_{t}^{\alpha}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the Euler equation is satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Transveraslity condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})k_{t+1}=0
\]

\end_inset

We want to show that the policy coverges to a steady state, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}=k_{t+1}=k^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, we even have global convergence so that everywhere in the state
 space converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{*}$
\end_inset

, but this will be proved formally later.
 At the steady state, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
k^{*} & = & \alpha\beta A\left(k^{*}\right)^{\alpha}\\
k^{*} & = & \left(\alpha\beta A\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}\\
\log k^{*} & = & \frac{\log\left(\alpha\beta A\right)}{1-\alpha}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The law of motion for the state variable is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\log k_{t+1}=\log(\alpha\beta A)+\alpha\log k_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

while at the steady state we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\log k^{*}=\log(\alpha\beta A)+\alpha\log k^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Subtracting equation (2) from equation (1) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\log k_{t+1}-\log k^{*} & = & \alpha\left(\log k_{t}-\log k^{*}\right)\\
 & = & \alpha^{2}\left(\log k_{t-1}-\log k^{*}\right)\\
 & \vdots\\
 & = & \alpha^{t+1}\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)\\
\log k_{t} & = & \log k^{*}+\alpha^{t}\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\log k_{t}$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\log k^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=(1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t}^{\alpha}\rightarrow c^{*}=(1-\alpha\beta)A\left(k^{*}\right)^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})k_{t+1}=\beta^{\infty}u^{\prime}(c^{*})k^{*}=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Example
Since both conditions are satisfied, then 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}=\alpha\beta Ak_{t}^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is the optimal allocation.
 In this example, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the patience level of the agent.
 A large 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 indicates that the consumer is more willing to save for the future.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\approx0$
\end_inset

, tomorrow's output is independent of today's savings, so the agent is more
 inclined to save less.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sequential Dynamic Programming
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our approach up to now has been to look for a sequence of real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\{k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 that generates an optimal consumption plan.
 An alternative approach is called 
\emph on
dynamic programming
\emph default
.
 The key to dynamic programming is to think of dynamic decisions being made
 not once and for all, but recursively, i.e., time period by time period.
 That is, the problem of
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{1},x_{2}}}\ f(x_{1},x_{2})
\]

\end_inset

is decomposed into subproblems:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{2}}}\ f(x_{1},x_{2}),\ \mbox{for fixed}\ x_{1}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x_{1}}\ f(x_{1},x_{2}^{*}(x_{1}))
\]

\end_inset

In a sense, we are decomposing the the problem and using backward induction
 to solve it.
 Note that this may not work for all problems, but in for the problems we
 solve, it does due to their special structure.
 Now, we will present two separate formulations for a dynamic programming
 problem.
 Note that many economic problems can be constructed both ways, but this
 may not always be the case.
 To achieve the best results, choose the setup that makes the most sense
 under the context of the problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dynamic Programming Presented in Ljungqvist and Sargent (2004)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 be a discount factor.
 We want to choose an infinite sequence of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

controls
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}r(x_{t},u_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=g(x_{t},u_{t})$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 given.
 We assume that the 
\emph on
per-period return function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x_{t},u_{t})$
\end_inset

 is concave and that the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{t+1},x_{t}):x_{t+1}\leq g(x_{t},u_{t}),u_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is convex and compact.
 Dynamic programming seeks a time-invariant 
\emph on
policy function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 mapping the 
\emph on
state
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 into the control 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

, such that the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{s}\}_{s=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 generated by iterating the two functions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
u_{t} & = & h(x_{t})\\
x_{t+1} & = & g(x_{t},u_{t})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

starting from initial condition 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

, solves the original problem.
 A solution in the form of equations (4) and (5) is said to be 
\emph on
recursive
\emph default
.
 To find the policy function 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 we need to know another function 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x)$
\end_inset

 that expresses the optimal value of the original problem, starting from
 an arbitrary initial condition 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

.
 This is called the 
\emph on
value function
\emph default
.
 In particular, define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*}(x_{0})={\displaystyle \max_{\{u_{s}\}_{s=0}^{\infty}}}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}r(x_{t},u_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where again the maximization is subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=g(x_{t},u_{t})$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 given.
 Of course, we cannot possibly expect to know 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x_{0})$
\end_inset

 until after we have solved the problem, but let's proceed on faith.
 If we knew 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x_{0})$
\end_inset

, then the policy function 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 could be computed by solving for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{u}}\ \left\{ r(x,u)+\beta V^{*}(\tilde{x})\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the maximization is subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}=g(x,u)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 given, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

 denotes the state next period.
 Thus, we have exchanged the original problem of finding an infinite 
\emph on
sequence
\emph default
 of controls that maximizes expression (3) for the problem of finding the
 optimal value function 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)$
\end_inset

 and a function 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 that solves the continuum of maximum problems (7), one maximum problem
 for each value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our task has become jointly to solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

, which are linked by the 
\emph on
Bellman equation
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*}(x)={\displaystyle \max_{u}}\ \left\{ r(x,u)+\beta V^{*}\left[g(x,u)\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The maximizer of the right side of equation (8) is a 
\emph on
policy function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

 that satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*}(x)=r\left[x,h(x)\right]+\beta V^{*}\left\{ g\left[x,h(x)\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (8) or (9) is a 
\emph on
functional equation
\emph default
 to be solved for the pair of unknown functions 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In many problems, there is no unique way of defining states and controls,
 and several alternative definitions lead to the same solution of the problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Here, we present two examples of formulating a dynamic programming problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example

\series bold
Tree Cutting Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose a farm has a tree of size 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 denote the initial size of the tree, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 be the the per-unit price of timber.
 In each period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the farmer can choose either to cut the tree and sell it for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x$
\end_inset

, or wait one more period.
 If the farmer waits, then the tree grows according to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=G(x_{t})$
\end_inset

, other wise 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}\cup\{0\}$
\end_inset

, and the state variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the size of the tree at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The the control variable 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes one of the two possible actions that can be taken, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=\left\{ C,NC\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 The transition of the state variable is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{t+1}=g(x_{t},u_{t})=\begin{cases}
G(x_{t}) & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}=NC\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}=C
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

while the per-period return function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r(x_{t},u_{t})=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}=NC\\
x_{t} & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}=NC
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
Ramsey Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The neoclassic growth model we have been studying can also be set up as
 a dynamic programming problem.
 The state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, and the state variable is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=k_{t}$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 can be the control variable.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=c_{t}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=g(x_{t},u_{t})\equiv f(x_{t})-c_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 is variable.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=k_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=g(u_{t})\equiv f(x_{t})-c_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 is constant.
 The per-period return function is just the utility function, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x_{t},u_{t})=U(c_{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under various particular assumptions about 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, it turns out that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The functional equation (8) has a unique strictly concave solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
This solution is approached in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $j\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 by iterations on
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{j+1}(x)={\displaystyle \max_{u}}\left\{ r(x,u)+\beta V_{j}(\tilde{x})\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}=g(x,u)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 given, starting from any bounded and continuous intial 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is a unique and time-invariant optimal policy of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=h(x_{t})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is chosen to maximize the right side of (6).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Off corners, the limiting value function 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 is differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the value function is differentiable, the first-order necessary condition
 for problem (7) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{u}(x,u)+\beta V^{*\prime}\left\{ g(x,u)\right\} g_{u}(x,u)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If we also assume that the policy function 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable, differentiation of expression (9) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*\prime}(x)=r_{x}\left[x,h(x)\right]+r_{u}\left[x,h(x)\right]h^{\prime}(x)+\beta V^{*\prime}\left\{ g\left[x,h(x)\right]\right\} \left\{ g_{x}\left[x,h(x)\right]+g_{u}\left[x,h(x)\right]h^{\prime}(x)\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When the states and controls can be defined in such a way that only 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 appears in the transition equation, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}=g(u)$
\end_inset

: the derivative of the value function becomes, after substituting expression
 (10) with 
\begin_inset Formula $u=h(x)$
\end_inset

 into (11)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*\prime}(x)=r_{x}\left[x,h(x)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is a version of a formula of Benveniste and Scheinkman (1979).
 Moreover, the first-order condition for the problem on the right side of
 the Bellman equation (expression (10)) in conjunction with the Beneveniste-Sche
inkman formula (expression (12)) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{u}(x_{t},u_{t})+\beta r_{x}(x_{t+1},u_{t+1})g^{\prime}(u_{t})=0,\ x_{t+1}=g(u_{t})
\]

\end_inset

where the first equation is a version of the Euler equation.
 Under circumstances in which the second equation can be inverted to yield
 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, using the second equation to eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 from the first equation produces a second-order difference equation in
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 since eliminating 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 brings in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example

\series bold
A sample Euler equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As an example of an Euler equation, consider the Ramsey problem.
 Let the state be 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and the control be 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 denotes next period's value of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 Substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $c=f(k)-k^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 into the utility function and express the Bellman equation as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*}(k)={\displaystyle \max_{\tilde{k}}}\left\{ u\left[f(k)-\tilde{k}\right]+\beta V^{*}(\tilde{k})\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Application of the Benveniste-Scheinkman formula gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{*\prime}(k)=u^{\prime}\left[f(k)-\tilde{k}\right]f^{\prime}(k)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Notice that the first-order condition for the maximum problem on the right
 side of equation (13) is 
\begin_inset Formula $-u^{\prime}\left[f(k)-\tilde{k}\right]+\beta V^{\prime}(\tilde{k})=0$
\end_inset

, which, using equation (14), gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u^{\prime}\left[f(k)-\tilde{k}\right]=\beta u^{\prime}\left[f(\tilde{k})-\hat{k}\right]f^{\prime}(k^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}$
\end_inset

 denotes the two-period-ahead value of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (15) can be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1=\beta\frac{u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})}{u^{\prime}(c_{t})}f^{\prime}(k_{t+1})
\]

\end_inset

an Euler equation that is exploited extensively in the theories of finance,
 growth, and real business cycles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dyanmic Programming Presented in Class
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Formally, a dynamic progamming problem is a problem with the following structure
: once can capture all relevant information for the decision maker in a
 state variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We have a state space 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, which must be low-dimension, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 is called a state variable.
 There are three notable characteristics that the state variable must satisfy:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 must be relevant to the optimizaiton problem at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and it must contain all the relevant information of the history.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Once the level of the state variable is known, it doesn't matter how it
 is obtained in the past (a state variable is a sufficient statistic).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=x_{s}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

, then the problem starting at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is the same as the problem starting at 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma(x_{t})$
\end_inset

 describes the feasibility set for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma:X\rightrightarrows X$
\end_inset

 is known as the feasibility correspondence, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}\in\Gamma(x_{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $F(x_{t},x_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is known as the per-period return function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The value function of the sequential problem is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V^{*}(x_{0})={\displaystyle \max_{\{x_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}F(x_{t},x_{t+1})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{t+1}\in\Gamma(x_{t})\\
 & x_{0}\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x_{0})$
\end_inset

 is a system of equations, and the number of equations equals the number
 of points in the state space.
 So, there is an equation associated with every point in the state space.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x_{0})$
\end_inset

 is necessary to find 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x_{0})$
\end_inset

, which is the optimal value function, but it is not sufficient since 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x_{0})$
\end_inset

 may not equal 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x_{0})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
We present the same two examples from before, but under the context of the
 new formulation that was just presented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example

\series bold
Tree Cutting Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose a farm has a tree of size 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 denote the initial size of the tree, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 be the the per-unit price of timber.
 In each period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the farmer can choose either to cut the tree and sell it for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x$
\end_inset

, or wait one more period.
 If the farmer waits, then the tree grows according to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=G(x_{t})$
\end_inset

, other wise 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}\cup\{0\}$
\end_inset

, and the state variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the size of the tree at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The feasibility correspondence is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Gamma(x_{t})=\begin{cases}
\left\{ 0,G(x)\right\}  & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}>0\\
\{0\} & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

while the per-period return function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(x_{t},x_{t+1})=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ x_{t+1}=G(x)\\
p\cdot x_{t} & \mbox{if}\ x_{t+1}=0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
Ramsey Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The neoclassic growth model we have been studying can also be set up as
 a dynamic programming problem.
 The state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, and the state variable is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=k_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The feasibility set is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}\in\left[0,f(x_{t})\right]\equiv\Gamma(x_{t})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x_{t})\equiv F(x_{t},1)+(1-\delta)x_{t}$
\end_inset

, and the per-period return function is 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x_{t},x_{t+1})=u(f(x_{t})-x_{t+1})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn our attention to the Bellman equaiton, which is also known as
 the functional equation.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(x_{0})={\displaystyle \max_{x_{1}\in\Gamma(x_{0})}}F(x_{0},x_{1})+\beta v(x_{1});\ \forall\ x_{0}\in X
\]

\end_inset

This represents a system of equations where the number of equations equals
 the number of unknown variables.
 Important to note, the value obtained from solving the problem 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

once and for all
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 equals the value obtained from the optimization of two decomposed parts.
 Solving the Bellman equation helps to fund the optimal policy funciton
 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

, which depends only on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V^{*}(x_{0})={\displaystyle \max_{x_{1}\in\Gamma(x_{0})}}F(x_{0},x_{1})+\beta V^{*}(x_{1}),\ \forall\ x_{0}\in X
\]

\end_inset

The collection of maximizers of the BE, 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{0})$
\end_inset

, is called the policy correspondence.
 When the maximizer is unique, it is called the policy function.
 As shown before, we can use the Bellman equation, along with the Envelope
 Theorem, to find the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 It is useful to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*\prime}(x)$
\end_inset

 because it can be used to help derive the steady state solution of the
 optimal path, and it is sometimes used in numerical methods to calculate
 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order condition of the right side of the BE is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{1}}+\beta V^{*\prime}(x_{1})=0;\ \forall\ x_{0}\in X
\]

\end_inset

Then, taking the derivative with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 and applying the Envelope Theorem, we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V^{*\prime}(x_{0}) & = & \frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{0}}+\frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{1}}h^{\prime}(x_{0})+\beta V^{*^{\prime}}(x_{1})h^{\prime}(x_{0})\\
 & = & \frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{0}}+\left[\frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{1}}+\beta V^{*^{\prime}}(x_{1})\right]h^{\prime}(x_{0})\\
V^{*\prime}(x_{0}) & = & \frac{\partial F(x_{0},x_{1})}{\partial x_{0}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=h(x_{0})$
\end_inset

.
 As shown in a past example, we can reproduce the Euler equation from the
 growth model using the results above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(Theorem 4.2 in Stokey, Lucas and Prescott (SLP)).

\series default
 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the functional equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let us consider the case of Cobb-Douglas transition and logarithmic preferences.
 We want to find the optimal value function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V^{*}(k_{0})={\displaystyle \sup_{\{k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\log(Ak_{t}^{\alpha}-k_{t+1}) & {\displaystyle }\\
\mbox{s.t.} & k_{t+1}\in\left[0,Ak_{t}^{\alpha}\right]\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and show that it satises the functional equation (FE).
 In the previous subsection, we proved that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(k_{t})=\alpha\beta Ak_{t}^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is the optimal policy function, and that it converges to a steady state,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $h(k^{*})=k^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 It was also shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\log k_{t}-\log k^{*}=\alpha^{t}\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 So, we can compute the value function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V^{*}(k_{0}) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\log(Ak_{t}^{\alpha}-k_{t+1})\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)Ak_{t}^{\alpha}\right)\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\alpha\log k_{t}\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\alpha\left[\log k^{*}+\alpha^{t}\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)\right]\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\log k^{*}}{1-\beta}+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\alpha\alpha^{t}\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\log k^{*}}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\left(\log k_{0}-\log k^{*}\right)}{1-\alpha\beta}\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\beta\log(\alpha\beta A)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\frac{\alpha\log(k_{0})}{1-\alpha\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is the left side of the Bellman equation.
 Now, we verify that it equals the right side
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{right side} & = & {\displaystyle \max_{k^{\prime}}\left\{ \log\left(Ak_{0}^{\alpha}-k^{\prime}\right)+\beta\left[\frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\beta\log(\alpha\beta A)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\frac{\alpha\log(k^{\prime})}{1-\alpha\beta}\right]\right\} }\\
 & = & \log\left((1-\alpha\beta)Ak^{\alpha}\right)+\beta\left[\frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\beta\log(\alpha\beta A)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\frac{\alpha\log(\alpha\beta Ak_{0}^{\alpha})}{1-\alpha\beta}\right]\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\alpha\log k_{0}+\beta\left[\frac{\alpha\beta\log(\alpha\beta A)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\frac{\alpha\log(\alpha\beta A)}{1-\alpha\beta}+\frac{\alpha^{2}\log k_{0}}{1-\alpha\beta}\right]\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\beta\log(A\alpha\beta)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\alpha\log k_{0}+\frac{\alpha^{2}\beta\log k_{0}}{1-\alpha\beta}\\
 & = & \frac{\log\left((1-\alpha\beta)A\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\alpha\beta\log(A\alpha\beta)}{(1-\beta)(1-\alpha\beta)}+\frac{\alpha\log k_{0}}{1-\alpha\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the optimal value function satisfies the Bellman equation (BE).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
In the example above, we began with the optimal policy function, then we
 derived the optimal value function, and finally, we verified 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 satisfied the BE.
 In general, we are unable to follow this progression.
 For most dynamic programming problems, we use the BE to derive the optimal
 value funciton, which express the optimal value of the original problem
 starting from an arbitrary initial condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(Theorem 4.3 in SLP).
 
\series default
\emph on
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 satisfies the FE and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 and for all feasible sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{t},\ldots)\in\Pi(x_{0})$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta^{t}v(x_{t})=0
\]

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula $v=V^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The transversality condition presented in Theorem 5 is much stronger than
 the one given in the previous section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is a bounded function that satisfies the FE, then 
\begin_inset Formula $v=V^{*}$
\end_inset

 and this bounded solution is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x_{t},x_{t+1})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma(x_{t})=\left[x_{t},x_{t+1}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 In this setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}=0$
\end_inset

, but the FE allows for other solutions besides 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 For instance, consider the following unbounded function that satisfies
 the FE
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V(x)=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ x=0\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ 0<x\leq1\\
\nicefrac{1}{\beta} & \mbox{if}\ 1<x\leq2\\
\left(\nicefrac{1}{\beta}\right)^{n} & \mbox{if}\ n<x\leq n+1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

However, 
\begin_inset Formula $v\neq V^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x)$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the transversality condition of Theorem 5.
 In this example, the FE has a continuum of solutions, but only 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 is bounded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Value Function Iteration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The main type of computation methods for solving dynamic programs aims to
 solve the functional equation.
 The primary method is known as 
\emph on
value function iteration 
\emph default
(VFI) or 
\emph on
iterating on the Bellman equation
\emph default
.
 It proceeds by constructing a sequence of value functions and associated
 policy functions.
 The sequence is created by iterating on the following equation, starting
 from 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset

, and continuing until 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{j}$
\end_inset

 has converged:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{j+1}(x)={\displaystyle \max_{u}}\left\{ r(x,u)+\beta V_{j}(\tilde{x})\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}=g(x,u)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The Contraction Mapper Operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T:\{v:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\}\rightarrow\{v:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\}$
\end_inset

 is a function on a state space 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $v\rightarrow Tv$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(Tv)(x)=\max_{y\in\Gamma(x)}F(x,y)+\beta v(y)
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $Tv=v$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is a fixed point of the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 satisfies the BE if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is a fixed point of operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now present three important properties of the contraction mapping operator.
 These properties rely on the sufficient condition that the per-period return
 function, 
\begin_inset Formula $F:X\times X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, is bounded.
 So, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ B>0,\forall\ (x,y)\in X\times X:|F(x,y)|\leq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 becomes a mapping from a bounded function to a bounded function, 
\begin_inset Formula $T:B(x)\rightarrow B(x)$
\end_inset

, where the metric space 
\begin_inset Formula $B(x)\equiv\left\{ v:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is a collection of bounded functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $P(Tv_{1},Tv_{2})\leq\beta\cdot P(v_{1},v_{2});\ \forall\ v_{1},v_{2}\in B(x)$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is the metric function defined on the metric space 
\begin_inset Formula $B(x)$
\end_inset

 that gives the distance between two functions, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $P(v_{1},v_{2})=\max_{x\in X}\left\Vert v_{1}(x)-v_{2}(x)\right\Vert $
\end_inset

.
 This property states that the distance between two transformed functions
 is less than or equal to the distance between the two original functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is a unique fixed point of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $B(x)$
\end_inset

, denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The assumption and its consequences are characterized in the following theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(VFI).

\series default
 
\emph on
If the per-period return function is bounded, then 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 maps from a bounded function to a bounded function, and 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{j}(v)$
\end_inset

 converges to a fixed point of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 uniformly for any bounded function 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

.
 This fixed point of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $B(x)$
\end_inset

 is unique and bounded, and hence, it is 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
We don't know 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

, so we need to establish a procedure to find it.
 First, we start with an aribtrary value function 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

, and then we iterate on operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
v_{0}\\
v_{0}=Tv_{0}\\
v_{2}=Tv_{1}=T(Tv_{0})=T^{2}v_{0}\\
\vdots\\
v_{n+1}=T(v_{n})=T(T^{n-1}v_{0})=T^{n}v_{0}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

We can think of VFI as a type of backward induction: 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is the value function of the last period, 
\begin_inset Formula $Tv$
\end_inset

 is the value function with 1 period left, 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{2}v$
\end_inset

 is the value function with 2 periods left, etc.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is just solving the maximization problem on the right side of the Bellman
 equation.
 According to Theorem 6, if the per-period function is bounded, then there
 exists a fixed point (a function) 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}P\left(T^{n}v,\bar{V}\right)=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left\{ \max_{x\in X}\left\Vert T^{n}v(x)-\bar{V}(x)\right\Vert \right\} =0$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the sequence of functions, 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{n}v$
\end_inset

, uniformly converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}=T\bar{V}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

is a fixed point of the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}=V^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset

 is a solution of the BE and it equals the optimal value function.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The number of iterations need to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 is unknown.
 Also, if the per-period return function is unbounded, then Theorem 6 does
 not hold, so the convergence of 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{n}(v)$
\end_inset

 is not guaranteed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Once again, we consider the growth model with Cobb-Douglas production and
 logarithmic preferences.
 Even though the utility function is unbounded, convergence still occurs.
 In previous examples, we have derived the optimal value function, the optimal
 policy function, and the steady state values.
 However, in this example, we show how to employ the method of VFI, as well
 as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Guess and Verify,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to achieve the desired results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let our initial guess be 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}(k)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we solve the one period problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Tv(k) & = & \max_{k^{\prime}\in\left[0,Ak^{\alpha}\right]}\log(Ak^{\alpha}-k^{\prime})+\beta v_{0}(k^{\prime})\\
 & = & \max_{k^{\prime}\in\left[0,Ak^{\alpha}\right]}\log(Ak^{\alpha}-k^{\prime})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is next period's value and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is this period's value of the capital stock.
 The solution is evidently to set 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}=0$
\end_inset

, which produces an optimized value
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
v_{1}(k) & = & \log A+\alpha\log k\nonumber \\
 & = & E_{1}+F_{1}\log k
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Now, using the same structure of equation (17), suppose
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{i}(k)=E_{i}+F_{i}\log k
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i}$
\end_inset

 are undetermined constants and 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{i}v_{0}(k)=v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The left and right sides of equation (18) must agree for all values 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 So, the next iteration on the BE is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{i+1}(k)={\displaystyle \max_{k^{\prime}}}\left\{ \log(Ak^{\alpha}-k^{\prime})+\beta\left[E_{i}+F_{i}\log k^{\prime}\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The first-order necessary conditions for the maximum problem on the right
 side of equation (19) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{Ak^{\alpha}-k^{\prime}}-\beta\frac{F_{i}}{k^{\prime}}=0
\]

\end_inset

which implies the following formula for the optimal policy 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}=h(k)$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
k^{\prime}=\frac{\beta F_{i}}{1+\beta F_{i}}Ak^{\alpha}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For the next iteration of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Tv_{i}(k)=E_{i+1}+F_{i+1}\log k
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Tv_{i}(k)=v_{i+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Substitute equation (20) into the Bellman equation (19) and equate the
 result to the right side of equation (21).
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{i+1}+F_{i+1}\log k=\log\left(\frac{A}{1+\beta F_{i}}\right)+\alpha\log k+\beta E_{i}+\beta F_{i}\log\left(\frac{\beta F_{i}A}{1+\beta F_{i}}\right)+\beta F_{i}\alpha\log k
\]

\end_inset

Solving the resulting equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E_{i+1} & = & \log\left(\frac{A}{1+\beta F_{i}}\right)+\beta F_{i}\log\left(\frac{\beta F_{i}A}{1+\beta F_{i}}\right)+\beta E_{i}\\
F_{i+1} & = & \alpha+\alpha\beta F_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

First, we solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
F_{0} & = & 0\\
\beta F_{1} & = & \alpha\beta\\
\beta F_{2} & = & \alpha\beta+(\alpha\beta)^{2}\\
 & \vdots\\
\beta F_{n} & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\left(\alpha\beta\right)^{i}\\
{\displaystyle \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta F_{n} & = & \frac{\alpha\beta}{1-\alpha\beta}\\
F_{\infty} & = & \frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Next, we solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E_{1} & = & \log A\\
E_{2} & = & \log\left(\frac{A}{1+\beta F_{1}}\right)+\beta F_{1}\log\left(\frac{A\beta F_{1}}{1+\beta F_{1}}\right)+\beta\log A\\
 & \vdots
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In Assignment 3, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}\ E_{n}=\frac{\log\left(\frac{A}{1+\beta F_{\infty}}\right)}{1-\beta}+\frac{\beta F_{\infty}\log\left(\frac{A\beta F_{\infty}}{1+\beta F_{\infty}}\right)}{1-\beta}
\]

\end_inset

Equation (20) shows that the optimal policy is to have capital move according
 to the difference equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t+1}=A\beta\alpha k_{t}^{\alpha}
\]

\end_inset

That 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is less than 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 converges as 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 approaches infinity for any positive initial value 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 The steady state, or stationary point, is given by the solution
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
k_{\infty} & = & A\beta\alpha k_{\infty}^{\alpha}\\
k_{\infty} & = & \left(A\beta\alpha\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In this example, we used VFI on the BE to obtain the optimal policy function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}=h(k)$
\end_inset

, and the optimal value function, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}(k_{0})$
\end_inset

, which is the proper method to solve a dynamic programming problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
In general, it is typically impossible analytically to compute even one
 iteration for VFI.
 This fact thrusts us into the domain of computational methods for approximating
 solutions: pencil and paper are insufficient
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Stochasic Control Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider a modification of problem (3) to permit uncertainty.
 Essentially, we add some well-placed shocks to the previous nonstochastic
 problem.
 So long as the shocks are either independently and identically distributed
 or Markov, straightforward modifications of the method for handling the
 nonstochastic problem will work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Markov Chain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\series bold
Markov chain
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s_{t}:t=1,2,\ldots\}$
\end_inset

 is a sequnce of random variables with a state space 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\bar{s}_{i}:i=1,2,\ldots,n\}$
\end_inset

 and with their transitional probability described by a transition matrix
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Pr}(s_{t+1}=\bar{s}_{j}|s_{t}=\bar{s}_{i})=\mathcal{P}_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\mbox{Pr}(s_{0})\ \mbox{initial condition}\\
\mbox{Pr}(s_{0},s_{1})=\mbox{Pr}(s_{0})\mbox{Pr}(s_{1}|s_{0})\\
\mbox{Pr}(s_{0},s_{1},s_{2})=\mbox{Pr}(s_{0})\mbox{Pr}(s_{1}|s_{0})\mbox{Pr}(s_{2}|s_{1})\\
\vdots
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The rows of the matrix signify the current state, while the columns are
 the future state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0.8 & 0.2\\
0.1 & 0.9
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Pr}(1)=\mbox{Pr}(2)=0.5$
\end_inset

, then compute 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Pr}(1,1,1)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Pr}(2,2,2)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Pr}(1,2,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\series bold
(A Markov chain is memoryless)
\series default
 A Markov chain satisfies the property that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Pr}(s_{t+1}|s_{t},s_{t-1},\ldots,s_{1},s_{0})\equiv\frac{\mbox{Pr}(s_{0},s_{1},\ldots,s_{t-1},s_{t},s_{t+1})}{\mbox{Pr}(s_{0},s_{1},\ldots,s_{t-1},s_{t})}
\]

\end_inset

does note depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $(s_{0},s_{1},\ldots,s_{t-1})$
\end_inset

, i.e., it is independent of the histories, which is different from other
 arbitrary stochastic processes.
 This 
\series bold
memoryless
\series default
 property is an equivalent definition of a Markov chain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
i.i.d.
 shocks is a Markov chain where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In general, however, Markov chains may not be able to be formulated as
 i.i.d.
 shocks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The value function of the sequential problem with uncertainty becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V^{*}(x_{0},s_{0})=\sup_{\{x_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}} & {\displaystyle E\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}F(x_{t},s_{t},x_{t+1})\right]}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{t+1}\in\Gamma(x_{t},s_{t})\\
 & x_{0},s_{0}\ \mbox{are given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and the Bellman equation can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V^{*}(x_{0},\bar{s}_{i})={\displaystyle \max_{x_{1}\in\Gamma(x_{0},\bar{s}_{i})}}F(x_{0},\bar{s}_{i},x_{1})+\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{n}}\mathcal{P}_{ij}V^{*}(x_{1},\bar{s}_{j});\ \forall\ x_{0},s_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Stochastic Dynamic Programming Presented in Ljungqvist and Sargent (2004)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, we modify the transition equation and consider the problem of maximizing
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\beta^{t}r(x_{t},u_{t})\right],\ 0<\beta<1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
