\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

, is strictly concave and strictly increasing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
strictly concave
\series default
 if for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 in the domain such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\neq x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\lambda x_{1}+(1-\lambda)x_{2})>\lambda f(x_{1})+(1-\lambda)f(x_{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(Strict Concavity).

\series default
 
\emph on
If the utility function is strictly increasing and strictly concave, and
 the production function, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(k,1)$
\end_inset

, is strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and strictly concave, then the optimal value function, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

, is strictly increasing and strictly concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We prove the two results separately by following three steps, respectively.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{*}$
\end_inset

 is strictly concave.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is weakly concave, then 
\begin_inset Formula $Tv$
\end_inset

