\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A steady state is a 
\emph on
constant
\emph default
 solution 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{*}$
\end_inset

, such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{0}=k^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t:k_{t}=k^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 At the steady state, 
\begin_inset Formula $h(k^{*})=k^{*}$
\end_inset

, which means that the steady state value of capital is the fixed point
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $h(k)$
\end_inset

.
 Consumption is also a constant sequence at the steady state.
 So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}=c^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t:c_{t}=c^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The steady state values of capital and consumption must satisfy the Euler
 equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\prime}(c^{*})=\beta u^{\prime}(c^{*})f^{\prime}(k^{*})
\]

\end_inset

which reduces to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
f^{\prime}(k^{*}) & = & \frac{1}{\beta}\\
F_{k}(k^{*},1) & = & \frac{1}{\beta}-(1-\delta)\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

This is the key condition for a steady state in the one-sector growth model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Once more, we consider the growth model with Cobb-Douglas transition and
 logarithmic preferences.
 The production function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(k)=Ak^{\alpha}
\]

\end_inset

the derivative with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f^{\prime}(k)=A\alpha k^{\alpha-1}
\]

\end_inset

and the inverse of the derivative is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(f^{\prime})^{-1}(k)=\left[\frac{1}{A\alpha}\left(k\right)\right]^{\frac{1}{\alpha-1}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

From our previous examples, we found the optimal policy function to be 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{\prime}=h(k)=\alpha\beta Ak^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, since 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is invertible, we can derive the steady state by using equation (27)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k^{*}=f^{\prime-1}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\right)=\left[A\alpha\beta\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}
\]

\end_inset

 or solving for the steady state using the optimal policy function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
k^{*} & = & h(k^{*})=\alpha\beta Ak^{*\alpha}\\
k^{*} & = & \left[A\alpha\beta\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Both methods lead to the unique steady state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
