\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To provide a better understanding of dynamic programming and the Bellman
 equation, we present an example of a job search model that uses the techniques
 of Dynamic Programming to derive a solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
McCall's Model of Intertemporal Job Search
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider an unemployed worker who is searching for a job under the
 following circumstances: Each period the worker draws one offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 from the same wage distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F(W)=\mbox{prob}\{w\leq W\}$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $F(0)=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(B)=1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $B<\infty$
\end_inset

.
 The worker has the option of rejecting the offer, in which case he or she
 receives 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 this period in unemployment compensation and waits until next period to
 draw another offer from 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

; alternatively, the worker can accept the offer to work at 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, in which case he or she receives a wage of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 per period forever.
 Neither quitting nor firing is permitted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 be the worker's income in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=c$
\end_inset

 if the worker is unemployed and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=w$
\end_inset

 if the worker is employed, or if he is unemployed and has accepted an offer
 to work at wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 The worker devises a strategy to maximize 
\begin_inset Formula $E\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}y_{t}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta<1$
\end_inset

 is a discount factor.
 Note that we can rewrite this model as a dynamic programming problem.
 The endogenous state space is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}\in\{E,U\}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 stands for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

employed,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 stands for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unemployed.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The exogenous state space is the i.i.d.
 wage offers 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w_{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 .
 The control variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

, is the decision of the worker to accept or reject the wage offer.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is the wage time time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, then the per-period return function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=r(x_{t},y_{t},u_{t})=\begin{cases}
w & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=E\\
w & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=U,u_{t}=\mbox{"accept"}\\
c & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=U,u_{t}=\mbox{"reject"}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the transition law is dictated by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(x_{t+1},w_{t+1})=g(x_{t},w_{t},u_{t},w_{t+1})=\begin{cases}
(E,w) & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=E\\
(E,w) & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=U,u_{t}=\mbox{"accept"}\\
(U,w_{t+1}) & \mbox{if}\ x_{t}=U,u_{t}=\mbox{"reject"}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

When a worker is employed, his non-stochastic optimal value function is
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(E,w)=w+\beta V(E,w)=\frac{w}{1-\beta}$
\end_inset

, so there is no need to solve the problem in this case.
 Hence, we will focus on the situation when the worker is unemployed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

 be the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}y_{t}$
\end_inset

 for an unemployed worker who has offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in hand, who is deciding whether to accept or to reject it, and who behaves
 optimally.
 We assume no recall.
 The value function 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Bellman equation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v(w)=\max\left\{ \frac{w}{1-\beta},c+\beta{\displaystyle \int}v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the maximization is over the two actions: (1) 
\emph on
accept
\emph default
 the wage offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and work forever at wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, or (2) 
\emph on
reject
\emph default
 the offer, receive 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 this period, and draw a new offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 from distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 next period.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 be the reservation wage of the worker, such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $w>\bar{w}$
\end_inset

, then the worker will accept the offer, or reject it otherwise.
 Thus, the solution of the functional equation (32) will be of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v(w)=\begin{cases}
\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}=c+\beta{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{B}}v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime}) & \mbox{if}\ w\leq\bar{w}\\
\frac{w}{1-\beta} & \mbox{if}\ w>\bar{w}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using equation (33), we can convert the functional equation (32) into an
 ordinary equation in the reservation wage 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
 Evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $v(\bar{w})$
\end_inset

 and using equation (33), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta} & = & c+\beta{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{B}}v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})\\
\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}\left({\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}dF(w^{\prime})+{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})\right) & = & c+\beta{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}dF(w^{\prime})+\beta{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}\frac{w^{\prime}}{1-\beta}dF(w^{\prime})\\
\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}dF(w^{\prime})-\beta{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}dF(w^{\prime})-c & = & \beta{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}\frac{w^{\prime}}{1-\beta}dF(w^{\prime})-\frac{\bar{w}}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})\\
\left(\frac{1}{1-\beta}-\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}\right)\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}dF(w^{\prime})-c & = & \frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}\beta w^{\prime}-\frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}\bar{w}dF(w^{\prime})\\
\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}dF(w^{\prime})-c & = & \frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}(\beta w^{\prime}-\bar{w})dF(w^{\prime})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Adding 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 to both sides gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\bar{w}}}dF(w^{\prime})+\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})-c & = & \frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}(\beta w^{\prime}-\bar{w})dF(w^{\prime})+\bar{w}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})\nonumber \\
\bar{w}\left({\displaystyle \int_{0}^{B}}dF(w^{\prime})\right)-c & = & \frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}(\beta w^{\prime}-\bar{w})dF(w^{\prime})+\frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}\bar{w}(1-\beta)dF(w^{\prime})\nonumber \\
\bar{w}-c & = & \frac{1}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}(\beta w^{\prime}-\bar{w}+\bar{w}-\bar{w}\beta)dF(w^{\prime})\nonumber \\
(\bar{w}-c) & = & \frac{\beta}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{\bar{w}}^{B}}(w^{\prime}-\bar{w})dF(w^{\prime})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Equation (34) is often used to characterize the determination of the reservation
 wage 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
 The left side is the cost of searching one more time when an offer 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 is in hand.
 The right side is the expected benefit of searching one more time in terms
 of the expected present value associated with drawing 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}>\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (34) instructs the agent to set 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 so that the cost of searching one more time equals the benefit of accepting
 the offer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us define the funciton on the right side of equation (34) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h(w)=\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}{\displaystyle \int_{w}^{B}}(w^{\prime}-w)dF(w^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(0)=\frac{E(w)\beta}{1-\beta}$
\end_inset

, that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(B)=0$
\end_inset

, and that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(w)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable, with derivative given by
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To compute 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime}(w)$
\end_inset

, we apply Leibniz' rule to equation (35).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(t)=\int_{\alpha(t)}^{\beta(t)}f(x,t)dx$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in[c,d]$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}$
\end_inset

 are continuouse and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are differentiable on 
\begin_inset Formula $[c,d]$
\end_inset

.
 Then, Leibniz' rule asserts that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(t)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable on 
\begin_inset Formula $[c,d]$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi^{\prime}(t)=f\left[\beta(t),t\right]\beta^{\prime}(t)-f\left[\alpha(t),t\right]\alpha^{\prime}(t)+{\displaystyle \int_{\alpha(t)}^{\beta(t)}}f_{t}(x,t)dx
\]

\end_inset

To apply this formula to the equation in the text, let 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 play the role of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{\prime}(w)=-\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}\left[1-F(w)\right]<0
\]

\end_inset

We also have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{\prime\prime}(w)=\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}F^{\prime}(w)>0
\]

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(w)$
\end_inset

 is decreasing and convex to the origin.
 It is apparent that an increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 leads to an increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Firing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now briefly consider a modifaction of the job search model in which each
 period after the first period on the job the worker faces probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 of being fired, where 
\begin_inset Formula $1>\alpha>0$
\end_inset

.
 The probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 of being fired next period is assumed to be independent of tenure.
 The worker continues to sample wage offers form a time-invariant and known
 probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and to receive unemployment compensation in the amount 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
 The worker receives a time-invariant wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 on a job until she is fired.
 A worker who is fired becomes unemployed for one period before drawing
 a new wage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We let 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

 be the expected present value of income of a previously unemployed worker
 who has offer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in hand and who behaves optimally.
 If she rejects the offer, she receives 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 in unemployment compensation this period and next period draws a new offer
 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, whose value to her now is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\int v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 If she rejects the offer, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)=c+\beta\int v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 If she accepts the offer, she recevies 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 this period, with proability 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\alpha$
\end_inset

 that she is not fired next period, in which case she receives 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta v(w)$
\end_inset

 and with proability 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 that she is fired, and after one period of unemployment draws a new wage,
 recieving 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\left[c+\beta\int v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if she accepts the offer, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)=w+\beta(1-\alpha)v(w)+\beta\alpha\left[c+\beta\int v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the Bellman equation becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(w)=\max\left\{ w+\beta(1-\alpha)v(w)+\beta\alpha\left[c+\beta Ev\right],c+\beta Ev\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Ev=\int v(w^{\prime})dF(w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 This equation has a solution of the form
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
That it takes this form can be established by guessing that 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

 is nondecreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 This guess implies the equation in the text for 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

, which is nondecreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 This argument verifies that 
\begin_inset Formula $v(w)$
\end_inset

 is nondecreasing, given the uniqueness of the solution of the Bellman equation.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(w)=\begin{cases}
\frac{w+\beta\alpha\left[c+\beta Ev\right]}{1-\beta(1-\alpha)} & \mbox{if}\ w>\bar{w}\\
c+\beta Ev & \mbox{if}\ w\leq\bar{w}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\bar{w}+\beta\alpha\left[c+\beta Ev\right]}{1-\beta(1-\alpha)}=c+\beta Ev
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The optimal policy is of the reservation wage form.
 The reservation wage 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 will not be characterized here as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
General Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we turn our attention to the decentralized market economy.
 We will show that the allocation achieved in decentralized trade will be
 the same as the allocation in the social planner's problem.
 In the social planner's economy, all variables were aggregate, while the
 decentralized market economy has individual and aggregate variables.
 For the decentralized market economy, we assume that each decision maker
 is a price-taker that has little influence on aggregate variables.
 Here is brief description of the different markets and their participants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Static Markets
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Goods Market: the price of the consumption good is normalized to 1, so it
 known as the numeraire.
 In this market, firms are sellers and households are buyers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Labor Market: the wage rate is 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is the price of labor at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 relative to (in terms of) consumption goods at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 In this market, households are sellers and firms are buyers.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Dynamic Market
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Financial Market: This market links periods together.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the real rate of return from saving 
\begin_inset Formula $(t-1\ \mbox{to}\ t)$
\end_inset

.
 In this market, households are sellers and firms are buyers.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Remark

\emph on
Exogenenous versus Endogenous Labor Supply:
\emph default
 Right now, the labor supply, 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}$
\end_inset

, is exogenously set to 1, but we will soon let it be chosen endogenously.
 In addition, we will later add growth to the model, as well as stochastic
 shocks that will affect productivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Household's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household's constrained maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle {\displaystyle \max_{\{k_{t+1}^{s},n_{t}^{s},c_{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}^{s}\leq w_{t}n_{t}^{s}+R_{t}k_{t}^{s};\ \forall\ t\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{s}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of labor supplied by the consumer in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{s}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of funds supplied to the financial market by the consumer
 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

.
 The right side of the consumer's budget constraint represents his income.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are two different scenarios we can consider for the model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Homogenous agent model: All consumers are identical, so this is a representative
-agent economy.
 The representative agent can save, but he can't borrow.
 So, we include the condition that 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{s}\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Heterogeneous agent model: Consumers are allowed to differ.
 In this model, borrowing is allowed, so the feasibility set for the consumer
 is larger than in the homogenous agent model.
 Thus, the budget constraint becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{t}+A_{t+1}\leq w_{t}n_{t}+R_{t}A_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 However, we must impose a non-ponzi game condition to prevent agents from
 borrowing forever.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}}\ \frac{A_{T+1}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}}\geq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Firm's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The firm wishes to maximize its total profit each period.
 So, its maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d}}}\ F(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{d}-w_{t}n_{t}^{d}-R_{t}k_{t}^{d}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is the amount of labor employed by the firm in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is the capital borrowed by the firm in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order conditions of the firm are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{t} & = & F_{k}(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})+(1-\delta)\\
w_{t} & = & F_{n}(k_{t}^{d},n_{t}^{d})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In this setup, we focus on the case where the production function satisfies
 constant returns to scale, so it is homogenous of degree one, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda>0:F(\lambda k,\lambda L)=\lambda F(k,L)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a mathematical result says we can write the production function as
 
\begin_inset Formula $F(k,L)=F_{k}\cdot k+F_{L}\cdot L$
\end_inset

.
 Under CRS, the firm's profit equals zero in a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The number of firms is undetermined as long as we have many small firms
 and the price-taking behavior can be justified.
 Because of CRS, having 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 firms is equivalent to having 1 firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Characterizing a Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium is a sequence of allocations, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{t}^{*},k_{t}^{s*},k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{s*},n_{t}^{d*}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, and prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{R_{t}^{*},w_{t}^{*}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Utility maximization
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\{c_{t}^{*},k_{t+1}^{s*},n_{t}^{s*}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}\in\arg\max{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ c_{t}^{*}+k_{t+1}^{s*}=w_{t}^{*}n_{t}^{s*}+R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{s*};\ \forall\ t\\
k_{0}^{s*}=k_{0}\ \mbox{is given}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Profit maximization
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{d*}\in\arg\max F(k_{t}^{d*},n_{t}^{d*})+(1-\delta)k_{t}^{d*}-w_{t}^{*}n_{t}^{d*}-R_{t}^{*}k_{t}^{d*}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}^{s*}=n_{t}^{d*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}^{s*}=k_{t}^{d*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the consolidated budget set of the household:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\frac{c_{t}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}+\frac{k_{T+1}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\frac{w_{t}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}+R_{0}^{*}k_{0}
\]

\end_inset

Under the non-ponzi game condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\ \frac{k_{T+1}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}\geq0
\]

\end_inset

the budget constraint in the limit is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\frac{c_{t}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\frac{w_{t}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}+R_{0}^{*}k_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
An allocation solves the household's problem if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{*}) & = & \beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{*})R_{t+1}^{*};\ \forall\ t\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\frac{c_{t}^{*}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}} & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{T}}\frac{w_{t}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}+R_{0}^{*}k_{0}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Equation (36) is the same as the transverality condition of the household
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\ \frac{k_{T+1}^{*}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Equation (36) is the Euler equation of the household, which keeps the household'
s consumption and saving smooth over time.
 It is equivalent to the Euler equation of the social planner, which is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\prime}(c_{t})=\beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})f^{\prime}(k_{t+1})
\]

\end_inset

 As stated in the Lemma, equation (38) is a type of transversality condition
 of the household, and it can be shown that it is equivalent to the transversali
ty condition of the social planner, which is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})k_{t+1}=0
\]

\end_inset

The interpretation of equation (38) is that the present value of the long-run
 savings is zero.
 For an allocation to be optimal, the transversality condition requires
 that a household must use all of its resources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\emph on
(First Welfare Theorem).
 
\series default
\emph default
The allocation in a competitive equilibrium solves the social planner's
 problem, i.e., it is Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose for contradiction that the social planner's allocation is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{0},k_{1}^{*},k_{2}^{*},\ldots
\]

\end_inset

 and the equilibrium allocaiton is different
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{0},k_{1},k_{2},\ldots;\ k_{1}\neq k_{1}^{*}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}<k_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then there is a contradiction to feasibility, which is explained below.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}>c_{o}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}>c_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}<k_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Recursively, it follows from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}>c_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}<k_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+1}>c_{t+1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}<k_{t+1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is large, 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{\prime}(k_{t})>\frac{\frac{1}{\beta}+1}{2}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}f^{\prime}(k_{t})=\frac{1}{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, it follows that from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}>c_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}<k_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{1}{\beta}+1}{2}(k_{t}^{*}-k_{t})<k_{t+1}^{*}-k_{t+1}
\]

\end_inset

This is a contradiction as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\frac{\frac{1}{\beta}+1}{2}\right)^{s}(k_{t}^{*}-k_{t})<k_{t+s}^{*}-k_{t+s}\leq k^{*}
\]

\end_inset

cannot hold for large 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 The left side approaches 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $s\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}>k_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then there is a contradiction to the agent's transverality condition,
 which is explained below.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}<c_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}<c_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}>k_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Recursively, from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}<c_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}>k_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we get 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+1}<c_{t+1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}>k_{t+1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Using the above technique, we can show that there exists a small 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 such that for large 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{t}^{*}+\epsilon<k_{t}
\]

\end_inset

Hence, the Euler equation implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\ c_{t}=0$
\end_inset

.
 It follolws that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\ k_{t}=\bar{k}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{k}$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\bar{k})=\bar{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}^{*}=f^{\prime}(k_{t})$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\ R_{t}^{*}=f^{\prime}(\bar{k})<1$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}}\ \Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}=0\\
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\ \frac{k_{T+1}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}=\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\ \frac{\bar{k}}{\Pi_{s=1}^{T}R_{s}^{*}}=\infty
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

contradicting the transversality condition of the household.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem

\series bold
\shape up
(Second Welfare Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
If an allocation is Pareto efficient (i.e., it solves the social planner's
 problem), then we can find prices such that the prices and the allocation
 constitute a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Endogenous Labor Supply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Social Planner's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We are changing the utility function to add 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

leisure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to the model.
 As a result, the labor supply will no longer be exogenously set to 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}:$
\end_inset

 labor supplied at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}:$
\end_inset

 leisure 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

consumed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}+L_{t}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With endogenous labor supply, the social planner's problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{k_{t+1},N_{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t},L_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}\leq F(k_{t},N_{t})+(1-\delta)k_{t}\\
 & N_{t}+L_{t}=1\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

so the Bellman equation is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V(k_{t})={\displaystyle \max_{N_{t},k_{t+1}}u(c_{t},1-N_{t})+\beta V(k_{t+1})}
\]

\end_inset

Setting up the Lagrangian, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left\{ u(c_{t},1-N_{t})+\lambda_{t}\left[F(k_{t},N_{t})+(1-\delta)k_{t}-c_{t}-k_{t+1}\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

and the first order conditions are listed below.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(c_{t}):\ u_{c}(t)-\lambda_{t}=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 known as the shadow price.
 It measures the marginal utility of consumption at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, i.e., the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of
 consumption.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(N_{t}):\ -u_{L}(t)+\lambda_{t}F_{N}(k_{t},N_{t})=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $-u_{L}(t)$
\end_inset

 is the marginal disutility of labor, i.e., it is the opportunity cost of
 losing a unit of leisure.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of consumptio
n, while 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{N}(k_{t},N_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the marginal product of labor (MPL).
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}F_{N}(k_{t},N_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the marginal utility product of labor, and it is approximately how much
 utility the agent gains when he works an extra hour.
 Thus, the marginal cost of labor equals the marginal benefit of labor.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(K_{t+1}):\ \lambda_{t}(-1)+E_{t}\left\{ \beta\lambda_{t+1}\left[F_{k}(k_{t+1},N_{t+1})+(1-\delta)\right]\right\} =0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(-\lambda_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the approximate reduction in utility if one unit of consumption is taken
 away for investment, i.e., the marginal cost of investment.
 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{k}(k_{t+1},N_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is the marginal product of capital (MPK).
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\delta)$
\end_inset

 tells us how much capital is left over after production.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 is the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of consumptio
n in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Because the expression on the right side of the equation takes place in
 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, we must multiply it by the discount factor, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Also, we take expectation of the right side because what happens in 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 is random.
 So, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left\{ \beta\lambda_{t+1}\left[F_{k}(k_{t+1},N_{t+1})+(1-\delta)\right]\right\} $
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the discounted expected marginal benefit of investment,
 measured in utility.
 Thus, the marginal cost of investment equals the discounted expected marginal
 benefit of investment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Along with the new transverality condition,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta^{t}\frac{\partial u(c_{t},L_{t})}{\partial c_{t}}k_{t+1}=0
\]

\end_inset

it can be shown that these conditions are necessary and sufficient.
 Thus, a solution to the social planner's problem must satisfy all of them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Consumer's Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A competitive equilibrium allocaiton must solve the consumer's constrained
 optimization problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{k_{t+1}^{s},N_{t}^{s}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(c_{t},L_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+k_{t+1}^{s}\leq R_{t}^{*}k_{t}+w_{t}^{*}N_{t}\\
 & N_{t}+L_{t}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Setting up the Lagrangian, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left\{ u(c_{t},1-N_{t})+\lambda_{t}\left[R_{t}^{*}k_{t}+w_{t}^{*}N_{t}-c_{t}-k_{t+1}\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

and the first order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial u(c_{t},1-N_{t})}{\partial c_{t}} & = & \lambda_{t}\\
-\frac{\partial u(c_{t},1-N_{t})}{\partial L_{t}} & = & \lambda_{t}w_{t}^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Facts About Growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the previous Ramsey models, growth was nonexistent since 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 converged to a steady state.
 We will now add growth to the Ramsey model.
 We will also formulare the model in such a way so that it mirrors the long-run
 macroeconomic data of the U.S.
 Here are some facts about empirical macroeconomic data since the Industrial
 Revolution.
 To simplify notation, total labor supply will be denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 for the time being.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
U.S.
 GDP grows at a constant rate.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(Y=F(K,L):\mbox{GDP net of depreciation})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The growth rate of output, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{y}$
\end_inset

, is a constant over time.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{y_{t+1}}{y_{t}}\approx\mbox{a constant})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
With the total labor supply normalized to 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, hours worked is roughly constant around 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

, and the average hours worked equals the total hours worked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Labor shares of income is a constant.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{WL}{Y}=\frac{2}{3})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The capital-labor ratio, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{K}{L})$
\end_inset

, grows at a constant rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The capital shares of income is a constant.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{K\cdot F_{K}(K,N)}{Y}\approx\mbox{a constant})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Capital-income ratio (capital-output ratio) is a constant (it does not depend
 on time).
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{K}{Y}\approx2.5\ \mbox{to}\ 3)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Real rates of return are constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Feasibility of Growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Feasibility Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The standard case is that of labor augmenting technological change:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}+I_{t}=F_{t}(K_{t},N_{t})=F(K_{t},\gamma^{t}N_{t})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{t}=K_{t+1}-(1-\delta)K_{t}$
\end_inset

 is investment at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 represents a constant returns to scale producation technology, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma>1$
\end_inset

 is the growth rate of efficiency units.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma^{t}n_{t}$
\end_inset

 signifies that the labor supply becomes more productive over time.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is chosen exogenously, and it represents better technology over time.
 So, we have the assumption that the production funciton is imporved from
 period to period.
 Thus, the feasibility set becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
C_{t}+K_{t+1}=\leq F(K_{t},\gamma^{t}N_{t})+(1-\delta)K_{t}\\
0\leq N_{t}\leq1
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Balanced Growth Path
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What kind of growth path do we need to have so the model is consistent with
 the data? Our object of study is called balanced growth: all economic variables
 grow at constant rates that could vary from one variable to another.
 For this path to be feasible (i.e., it's an element of the set above), all
 the variables must grow at the same rate, except 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 since it is bounded between 0 and 1.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Y_{t} & = & y_{0}g_{y}^{t}\\
C_{t} & = & c_{0}g_{c}^{t}\\
K_{t} & = & k_{0}g_{k}^{t}\\
I_{t} & = & i_{0}g_{i}^{t}\\
N_{t} & = & n_{0}g_{n}^{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{n}$
\end_inset

 cannot exceed 1, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{n}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the capital accumulation law,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
K_{t+1}=(1-\delta)K_{t}+I_{t}\\
g_{i}=\frac{I_{t+1}}{I_{t}}=\frac{K_{t+2}-(1-\delta)K_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}-(1-\delta)K_{t}}=g_{k}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

If both 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}$
\end_inset

 grow at a constant rate, it must be the case that they both grow at the
 same rate, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{k}=g_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By the same type of reasoning, from the resource constraint,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
C_{t}+I_{t}=Y_{t}\\
g_{i}=\frac{I_{t+1}}{I_{t}}=\frac{Y_{t+1}-C_{t+1}}{Y_{t}-C_{t}}=\frac{(Y_{t}-C_{t})g_{y}}{Y_{t}-C_{t}}=g_{y}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

we must have that 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{y}=g_{c}=g_{i}=g_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Next, using the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 represents a constant-returns-to-scale technology, we have that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(K_{t},N_{t}\gamma^{t})=Y_{t}=y_{0}g_{y}^{t}=F(k_{0},n_{0})g_{y}^{t}=F(K_{t},n_{0}g_{k}^{t})
\]

\end_inset

Hence, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=g_{k}$
\end_inset

, so all other variables also grow at rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Nature of Technological Change
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the analysis in the previous section, it seems natural to ask whether
 the assumption that the technological change is labor augmenting is relevant
 or not.
 First, what other kinds of technological change? Let us write the economy's
 resource constraint with all the possible types of technological progress
 taht the literature has talked about:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}+\frac{I_{t}}{g_{i}^{t}}=g_{z}^{t}F(g_{k}^{t}K_{t},g_{n}^{t}N_{t})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
If the production funciton is Cobb-Douglas, then all of the above technology
 changes are equivalent to labor-augmenting growth after change of variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $F(\gamma^{t}K_{t},N_{t})=\left(\gamma^{t}K_{t}^{\alpha}\right)N_{t}^{1-\alpha}=K_{t}^{\alpha}\left(\left[\gamma^{\left(\nicefrac{\alpha}{1-\alpha}\right)}\right]^{t}N_{t}\right)^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\gamma}=\gamma^{\left(\nicefrac{\alpha}{1-\alpha}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}^{\alpha}(\tilde{\gamma}_{t}N_{t})^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma^{t}F(K_{t},N_{t})=\gamma_{t}K_{t}^{\alpha}N_{t}^{1-\alpha}=K_{t}\left(\gamma^{\left(\nicefrac{t}{1-\alpha}\right)}N_{t}\right)^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\gamma}=\gamma^{\left(\nicefrac{1}{1-\alpha}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}+\left(\frac{I_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}\right)=F(K_{t}N_{t})=C_{t}+\frac{K_{t+1}-(1-\delta)K_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}=K_{t}^{\alpha}N_{t}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{K}_{t}=\frac{K_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}+\gamma\tilde{K}_{t+1}=(\tilde{K}_{t}\gamma^{t})^{\alpha}N_{t}^{1-\alpha}+(1-\delta)\tilde{K}_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The specific 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 in front of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 determines the growth rate of the variables.
 If the production function is not Cobb-Doublas, then only
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}+K_{t+1}=F(K_{t},\gamma^{t}N_{t})+(1-\delta)K_{t}
\]

\end_inset

is consistent with a balanced growth path.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Choosing Growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The problem faced by the social planner will be of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(C_{t},1-N_{t})\\
 & C_{t}+K_{t+1}\leq F(K_{t},\gamma^{t}N_{t})+(1-\delta)K_{t}\\
 & 0\leq N_{t}\leq1\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

As we showed in the previous subsection, a balanced growth path is consistent
 (i.e., feasible) with the constraint.
 For the balanced growth path to be optimal, we must impose restrictions
 on the utility funciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Balanced growth is possible as a solution to the social planner's problem
 if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C,L)=\frac{C^{1-\sigma}v(L)}{1-\sigma};\ \sigma>0
\]

\end_inset

where time endowment is normalized to one as usual and 
\begin_inset Formula $v(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a function with leisure, 
\begin_inset Formula $L=1-N$
\end_inset

, as an argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is an arbitrary function, but it must be concave and increasing.
 Another common utility function is the King-Plosser-Rebello
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C,L)=\begin{cases}
\frac{1}{1-\sigma}\left(\left[C\nu(L)\right]^{1-\sigma}-1\right) & \mbox{if}\ \sigma\neq1\\
\ln C+v(L) & \mbox{if}\ \sigma=1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Transforming the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How do we solve the new social planner's problem? We define the transformed
 variables to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

eliminate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 growth from the model.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t} & = & \frac{C_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}\\
i_{t} & = & \frac{I_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}\\
k_{t} & = & \frac{K_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}\\
y_{t} & = & \frac{Y_{t}}{\gamma^{t}}\\
N_{t} & = & N_{t}\\
\hat{\beta} & = & \beta\gamma^{1-\sigma}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

deflating
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the variables.
 The transformed variables listed above will converge to a steady state.
 Thus, obtain the transformed model.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t},N_{t},K_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{t}}\hat{\beta}^{t}\frac{c_{t}^{1-\sigma}v(1-N_{t})}{1-\sigma}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+\gamma k_{t+1}=F(k_{t},N_{t})+(1-\delta)k_{t}\\
 & 0\leq N_{t}\leq1\\
 & k_{0}>0\ \mbox{is given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

For this problem to be well defined, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\gamma^{1-\sigma}<1$
\end_inset

 is a necessary requirment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

